A||||7||WisconsinGT||Tran. filter||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||06-09-2000||11:59 AM||||I am going to replace mine. (88GT Auto.) Is it as simple as it looks? Or as the Haynes manual states...I need to replace a bolt. Is this true? I know I have to take off the pan in order to drain the oil. Then take the filter out. Put in the new filter then put the new gasket in. But do I use all of the old bolts? Or do I have to get a new set of bolts as well? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||06-09-2000||12:13 PM||||The Chilton "bolt" thing is so you can avoid a mess when dropping the pan. <P>Best way to do this is to pump out as much of the old tranny fluid through the dipstick tube as you can. That way the fluid level is low enough that the pan can be dropped without leaving two or three quarts of ATF all over your garage floor. Other than the mess, this is a pretty straightforward job that shouldn't take more than a half hour to do.<P>The filter fits into a rubber sleeve that attaches to the valve body and is held by a metal tab that the filter fits under. Don't reuse the old rubber sleeve...be sure to remove it if it doesn't come off with the filter and insert the new sleeve before inserting the filter. Get a good quality kit with a rubber gasket and yes...the old bolts are okay to reuse.||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||06-09-2000||12:15 PM||||Oh...and don't forget to clean the pan and magnet...and don't forget to put the magnet back where it belongs. <P>You'll see what I mean when you drop the pan.||||reg||1
Z||000003||triker||06-09-2000||02:56 PM||||I just changed mine and didn't spill a drop.  I put a little 12"x12" plastic dish wash tub under the poined end of the trans pan and started removing bolts from that end.  Fluid started leaking out after I'd removed about 6 bolts.  I kept working my way towards the square end, letting the pan drop a little more with each pair of bolts (opposite sides).  When I got to the ones accross the square end, I loosened them a little at a time until the pan was hanging at about a 30 degree angle.  Then I pushed it back up level with one hand while I removed the last few bolts with the other.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||06-09-2000||04:50 PM||||Thanks guys! Well, I went to AutoZone last week and purchased a filter. It has the filter and a cork gasket. Should I take it back? What is the brand name I should be looking for that has this "rubber sleeve" thing you speak of? Everything else looks straight forword. Cheers!||||reg||1
Z||000005||GT Bastard||06-09-2000||05:05 PM||||I wouldn't worry about it Wisconsin.  If they designed it that way, it should work.  Mind was kindof a hard rubber on the piece that enters the valve body of the tranny.  It just snaps into place.||||reg||1
Z||000006||deceler8||06-09-2000||06:29 PM||||Funny...I got my last filter from AutoZone and it had a rubber, not cork gasket.<P>The rubber sleeve thing is probably already on the end of your new filter. <P>Cork is okay, I just prefer rubber. Usually stays in place better while being installed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||80's BOY||06-09-2000||08:08 PM||||just joined in....something i learned. the magnet is REMOVEABLE from the bottom of the pan. don't waste your time cleaning it while it is on the pan like i did. take it off and run it under some high water pressure or parts cleaner. then put it back.||||reg||14
